Developer Guide
===============

Getting Started
---------------

Install Java 8.

Install Typesafe Activator 1.3.12 [https://downloads.typesafe.com/typesafe-activator/1.3.12/typesafe-activator-1.3.12-minimal.zip].

Run `activator run` to start the web app on http://localhost:9000/.

Alternatively, run with Docker (requires zenobase-cookbooks to be checked out alongside this repo):

  docker/build.sh
  docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.local.yml up

This starts both the Play app (port 9000) and Elasticsearch (port 9200) in containers.


Updating Assets
---------------

When updating the bootstrap.js file, concatenate the "Modals", "Dropdowns", "Toggleable tabs" and the "Typeahead" plugins,
and append the bootstrap-fileupload, bootstrap-datepicker and bootstrap-timepicker plugins.

When updating angular-custom.js, append angular-route and angular-sanitize. Remove any "//# sourceMappingURL" lines.

When updating highcharts.js, append highcharts-more.js and modules/exporting.js.

When updating moment.js, append moment-timezone-all-years.js.

When updating Font Awesome, use the unminified css version as the less file, and copy the font files to public/fonts/.

When updating highlight.js, create a custom package with support for bash and json, and copy styles/railscasts.css to
app/assets/css/highlight-railscasts-x.y.less.


Running Tests
-------------

To run all unit tests:

  activator "test-only *Test"

To run a specific test:

  activator "test-only com.zenobase.testing.BrowserTesting"

To skip webdriver tests, set -Dwebdriver.skip=true

To test specific tasks manually:

  1. Find the appropriate *Testing class, e.g. FitbitTesting.

  2. Set up a launch configuration with appropriate oauth.apiKey and oauth.apiSecret system properties.

  3. Run the test. Open the provided URL, complete the authorization, and paste the final URL back into the console.
     Note that IntelliJ has to be started with -Deditable.java.test.console=true in order for this step to work.

  4. Verify the result in the console. Copy the provided oauth.token and oauth.secret system properties into the launch
     configuration to bypass the authorization step in future test runs.


Profiling
---------

1. Run the JVM that will be monitored with the following properties:

   -Dcom.sun.management.jmxremote \
   -Dcom.sun.management.jmxremote.port=1099 \
   -Dcom.sun.management.jmxremote.rmi.port=1099 \
   -Dcom.sun.management.jmxremote.authenticate=false \
   -Dcom.sun.management.jmxremote.ssl=false

2. Create an ssh tunnel (with dynamic port forwarding and listening on port 1999) to the remote machine.

3. Configure jvisualvm or jmc to use localhost:1999 as a SOCKS proxy.

4. Create a JMX connection using the *internal* IP address of the remote machine, and port 1099.


Deploy Procedure
----------------

Normal deploys (code changes only) are fully automated:

  1. Push to master.

  2. GitHub Actions runs tests, builds Docker images, pushes to ECR.

  3. The deploy workflow runs Pulumi to create a new EC2 instance in the inactive
     target group. ES on the new instance joins the existing cluster via unicast
     and replicates data automatically.

  4. Once the ALB health check passes, the listener switches to the new target group.

  5. The old instance is terminated after the drain period.


Release Procedure
-----------------

Use this procedure when there are infrastructure or configuration changes:

  1. Update secrets in Secrets Manager if needed.

  2. Update Pulumi config (infra/Pulumi.prod.yaml) if needed.

  3. Sign in to AWS and export credentials:

       aws login
       eval $(aws configure export-credentials --format env)

  4. Run `pulumi up` from the infra/ directory to apply changes.

  5. Push code to master to trigger the CI/CD pipeline.


Migration Procedure
-------------------

Use this procedure to rebuild the database (e.g. after data model changes):

  1. Note the current EC2 instance's private IP.

  2. Set migration config:

       cd infra
       pulumi config set zenobase:esCluster "<new-cluster-name>"
       pulumi config set zenobase:esReplayCluster "<old-cluster-name>"
       pulumi config set zenobase:esReplayHost "<old-instance-ip>"
       pulumi config set zenobase:oldInstanceIp "<old-instance-ip>"

  3. Run `pulumi up` to create the new instance (do NOT switch the ALB yet).

  4. The new instance starts with an empty ES cluster. The Play app connects to the
     old cluster via es.replay.host and replays all commands.

  5. Monitor replay progress in CloudWatch Logs.

  6. When replay completes, verify data on the new instance.

  7. Switch the ALB to the new target group (via Pulumi config or AWS console).

  8. Terminate the old instance.

  9. Clear migration config:

       pulumi config set zenobase:esReplayCluster ""
       pulumi config set zenobase:esReplayHost ""
       pulumi config set zenobase:oldInstanceIp ""


Restore Procedure
-----------------

  1. Bring up an empty elasticsearch instance.

  2. Register a snapshot repository, e.g.

    curl -XPUT 'localhost:9200/_snapshot/prod-xx' -d '{
      "type": "s3",
      "settings": {
          "bucket": "zeno-snapshots",
          "base_path": "prod-xx"
      }
    }'

  3. List snapshots:

    curl 'localhost:9200/_snapshot/prod-xx/_all?pretty=true'

  4. Restore a snapshot:

    curl -XPOST 'localhost:9200/_snapshot/prod-xx/yyyyyyyyyy/_restore'

  5. Monitor progress:

    curl 'localhost:9200/_cat/recovery'
